index 0000000..39ed4a2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/PRIVACY.md
@@ -0,0 +1,117 @@
+# Privacy by design — The Daily Grind website
+
+This document is the engineering record of how the site protects visitors' privacy.
+It covers the markup in `index.html`, the client script in `assets/app.js`, the
+response headers in `_headers` / `package.json`, and the obligations this page places
+on the backend endpoints it talks to. `npm run check:privacy` enforces the technical
+parts automatically.
+
+## 1. Principles
+
+| Principle | How the site applies it |
+| --- | --- |
+| Data minimisation | The homepage collects nothing passively: no cookies, no analytics, no fingerprinting, no third-party requests. The only data collected is one email address, sent only when a visitor explicitly submits the newsletter form. |
+| Purpose limitation | The newsletter form states its single purpose inline ("Why we ask"). The backend must not reuse the address for anything else (see §5). |
+| Transparency | Explanations sit next to the field, not in a policy page. The footer states that the page sets no cookies and loads no third parties, and links to "Manage your data". |
+| Secure by default | Strict CSP, Trusted Types, clickjacking protection, strict referrer policy, powerful browser features disabled, HSTS, no-store caching for personal pages. |
+| No privacy labour | Because nothing is tracked, there is no consent banner to click through. Adding a tracker would require adding a consent flow; do not do the former without the latter. |
+
+## 2. Data inventory (homepage)
+
+| Data | Collected when | Sent to | Stored in browser | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Email address | Visitor submits the newsletter form | `POST /api/newsletter` (same origin, no cookies needed) | Never. The field is cleared from the DOM after a successful submit. | Monthly menu email, double opt-in required |
+| Session cookie (if signed in) | Set by the account backend, not by this page | Same origin only | `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax` (set server-side) | Authentication |
+| Nothing else | — | — | — | — |
+
+Client-side storage (`localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, IndexedDB) and `document.cookie`
+are never written by site scripts; `assets/app.js` only *clears* web storage on logout.
+
+## 3. Third-party audit
+
+Audited 2026-10-28 by inspecting every absolute URL in HTML, CSS, and JS.
+
+| Origin | What it was | Data a visitor would leak | Decision |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `images.unsplash.com` | Hero background image loaded by CSS | IP address, `Referer`, user agent, and page-view timing on every visit | **Removed.** The photo (Unsplash photo `1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085`, Unsplash License) is now self-hosted at `assets/images/hero-coffee.jpg`. |
+
+Result: **zero third-party origins**. `ALLOWED_THIRD_PARTY_ORIGINS` in
+`tools/privacy-check.mjs` is empty and the check fails on any new external URL.
+
+### Adding a third party later
+
+1. Record it in the table above: origin, purpose, exact data sent, retention, and the
+   vendor's privacy terms. Prefer a self-hosted or first-party alternative first.
+2. Prefer static links over SDKs (e.g. a plain share link instead of a social widget)
+   and a click-to-load façade for heavy embeds (e.g. `youtube-nocookie.com` only after
+   the visitor clicks a thumbnail).
+3. Pin to a versioned, immutable URL and add `integrity="sha384-…"`,
+   `crossorigin="anonymous"`, and `referrerpolicy="no-referrer"` on the element.
+4. Add the origin to `ALLOWED_THIRD_PARTY_ORIGINS` and to the relevant CSP directive
+   in all three places (`_headers`, `package.json`, `<meta>`), then run the check.
+5. If it collects personal data or sets cookies, add a consent flow before shipping.
+
+## 4. Security headers
+
+Sent for every response. Local dev gets the CSP via `statikk --csp` (the only header
+the dev server can set); production gets the full set from `_headers`
+(Netlify / Cloudflare Pages format — port the same values to any other host). The
+CSP and referrer policy are duplicated as `<meta>` tags in `index.html` as a fallback
+for hosts without header control.
+
+| Header | Value | Why |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `Content-Security-Policy` | `default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'none'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; require-trusted-types-for 'script'` | Only same-origin code and assets can load; forms can only post to this origin, so an injected form cannot exfiltrate the email; no inline script/style, so XSS payloads do not execute; Trusted Types blocks DOM XSS sinks at runtime. |
+| `Referrer-Policy` | `strict-origin-when-cross-origin` | Paths and query strings never leak to other origins. |
+| `Permissions-Policy` | camera, microphone, geolocation, motion sensors, display capture, USB, MIDI, XR, Topics/FLoC all `()` | Powerful and tracking-adjacent features are off for this site and any iframe. `payment` and `publickey-credentials-get` are left enabled because the ordering and account pages may legitimately use them. |
+| `X-Frame-Options: DENY` + CSP `frame-ancestors 'none'` | | Clickjacking protection. |
+| `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` | | Prevents MIME sniffing of responses. |
+| `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy` | `same-origin-allow-popups` | Isolates the window from cross-origin openers while still allowing sign-in popups the site itself opens. |
+| `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy` | `same-origin` | Other sites cannot embed or read our assets. |
+| `Strict-Transport-Security` | `max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains` | Forces HTTPS. See the rollout note in `_headers` before enabling on a new domain. |
+| `Clear-Site-Data` (on `/logout`) | `"cookies", "storage", "cache"` | Leaves nothing behind on shared devices. `assets/app.js` also clears web storage client-side as a fallback. |
+| `Cache-Control: no-store` (on `/logout`, `/account/*`, `/api/*`) | | Personal responses never land in shared caches. |
+
+No reporting endpoint is configured yet. When one exists, add
+`Reporting-Endpoints: default="https://…"` and `report-to default` to the CSP in
+`_headers`, and make sure the endpoint strips query strings and cookies from reports.
+
+## 5. Requirements for the endpoints this page uses
+
+The page is static, so these controls live in the backend. They are part of the
+design and must hold before the endpoints go live.
+
+**`POST /api/newsletter`**
+- Accept only the `email` field; reject or drop anything else.
+- Double opt-in: send a confirmation link and store the address only after it is clicked;
+  delete unconfirmed addresses after a short window (e.g. 48 hours).
+- Rate-limit by IP and by address to prevent abuse and enumeration.
+- Never log the address in plain text; mask PII in application logs.
+- CSRF: reject cross-site requests using Fetch Metadata (`Sec-Fetch-Site` not in
+  `same-origin`/`none`) before doing any work. The static page cannot carry a token.
+- Respond with `Cache-Control: no-store` and `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin`.
+- One-click unsubscribe in every email (`List-Unsubscribe` / `List-Unsubscribe-Post`).
+
+**`POST /logout`**
+- Accept POST only (the page no longer uses a GET link).
+- Validate `Sec-Fetch-Site` as above, invalidate the session server-side, expire the
+  cookie, and send `Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage", "cache"`.
+
+**Session cookies** (set by `/account`, not this page)
+- `__Host-session=…; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/`. Never readable by
+  script, never `SameSite=None`.
+
+**Data rights** (`/account/data`, linked from the footer)
+- Let signed-in users export and delete their data without contacting support, and
+  let newsletter subscribers delete their address via the unsubscribe link.
+
+## 6. Running the check
+
+```sh
+npm run check:privacy
+```
+
+It fails the build if any file references an unaudited external origin, uses a DOM
+XSS sink (`innerHTML`, `document.write`, `eval`, …), uses inline scripts/styles/event
+handlers, writes cookies or web storage, submits a form via GET, or if the CSP in
+`package.json`, `_headers`, and `index.html` drift apart or lose a required directive.
